# Security headers for header-capable static hosts / CDNs.
#
# GitHub Pages ignores this file and cannot send custom response
# headers, so on Pages the Content-Security-Policy and Referrer-Policy
# are delivered via <meta> tags from the respective root layouts instead. Placing
# a CDN that honours _headers (or equivalent header rules) in front of
# the site activates everything below, including the directives a meta
# tag cannot express. This is the shared ceiling for both root layouts:
# the portfolio meta policy restricts media to 'self', while the personal
# root permits decrypted blob media and restricts connections to 'self'.
# Browsers intersect header and meta policies; a shared header must permit
# both roots' resources. Keep these policies aligned with src/lib/security.ts.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://*.posthog.com; script-src-attr 'none'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self' blob:; worker-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://*.posthog.com https://formspree.io; form-action 'self' https://formspree.io; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
